# -*- mode: org -*-
#+TITLE: The queue for the stdcxx revival
#+STARTUP: overview

The queue, in priority order, top first. Each entry states the fact,
the intended fix, and the check that settles it. Finished entries
leave this file; git remembers them. The notes under
=doc/notes/working= carry the longer investigations.

* TODO 21.cwchar aborts after passing

All 65 assertions pass and the process dies at exit with "double
free or corruption (top)".

Fix: find the corruption; a memory checker run is the first step.

Check: exit 0.

* TODO Locale facets

=22.locale.num.get= fails 204 assertions, the =wchar_t= overloads
reading =unsigned long= consume the wrong count; =22.locale.time.get=
fails 33, =get_date ("01/01/2000")= consumes 10 characters where 8
are expected and =get_weekday ("torsdag")= is not recognized;
=22.locale.money.get= fails 16, =long double= reads with =showbase=
end in =failbit=.

Fix: per facet, after reading each test against the library.

Check: the three rows at 100% on both widths.

* TODO Numerics

=18.numeric.special.float= reports =has_denorm == 1= where the test
expects =-1= (=denorm_indeterminate=) for =float= and =long double=,
3 assertions in 64-bit and 6 in 32-bit with GCC, 3 in both with
Clang, whose 32-bit build gives the 64-bit row; =26.c.math= fails the
=modf= overloads for =float= and =long double= in 64-bit only;
=26.valarray.transcend= fails 3 in 32-bit only; =17.extensions=
fails 5 of 16 on =_RWSTD_NO_EXT_FAILURE= and its kin.

Fix: each is a characterization question first, whether the config
test or the test's expectation is out of date.

Check: the rows at 100% on both widths.

* TODO Test-case structs: size_t fields, NPOS sentinel

=StringTestCase= (=tests/include/rw_strings.h=) and =ContainerTestCase=
(=tests/include/rw_containers.h=) hold positions and counts in =int=
fields, and every table spells "not applicable" as =-1=, which reaches
the library as =size_t= and lands as =npos=: the sentinel and =npos=
are the same number by design.

Fix, as one inert commit after the baseline: retype =off=, =size=,
=off2= and =size2= to =_RWSTD_SIZE_T= in both structs, and change the
sentinel to =NPOS= in those four positions across the 171 =TEST= macro
bodies and the rows that pass it; =val= and =bthrow= stay =int= with
their own =-1=.

Check: identical assertion counts for every string and container test
before and after.

* TODO The wide half of UserCtype

=tests/src/ctype.cpp= implements =UserCtype<wchar_t>= and
=UserCtype<UserChar>= only in part: ten members are "FIXME: implement
this" stubs, and =make_char= (=tests/include/rw_char.h=) takes only a
=char=. =tests/self/0.ctype.cpp= carries the matching FIXME and breaks
out of its table at =UCHAR_MAX=, so the wide entries of its character
table have never been exercised on any platform.

Fix: finish the helper, give =make_char= an =int= overload, remove the
break.

Check: =0.ctype= exercises the whole table for all three character
types.

* TODO README section 5 and the configuration

The original README recommends setting =CPPOPTS= and =CXXOPTS= on the
make command line to change compiler options. The configuration
directory is insulated from those variables by design: =config.h= is a
function of the compiler, its version and the build type, and
anything the configuration must see goes in the =CONFIG= file. A
dialect option passed only on the command line produces a =config.h=
for one language and a library built in another.

Fix: document, not patch; the per-platform manual step the README
describes is a defect of the configuration to move away from, not a
behaviour to propagate. =README.md= already says so; the original
README stays as it was.

Check: none; this is prose.

* TODO std::locale MT-safety

=22.locale.numpunct.mt= fails intermittently. Hypotheses: unlocked
reads of the global and classic locale bodies, a facet-id race, or a
memory-ordering assumption that x86 hides.

Instruments: a ThreadSanitizer build of the library and the MT tests
first, now that it reproduces here; then real weakly-ordered hardware
for the ordering hypothesis, never an emulator.

It is not intermittent any more. In =15D= on x86-64 the locale MT
tests, =22.locale.codecvt.mt= through =22.locale.time.put.mt=, end
differently on every run of the same binary: three bare runs of
=22.locale.numpunct.mt= died on its own assertion in a thread (a
thread reading another locale's =truename=, line 147), died of an
uncaught =std::runtime_error= from a locale that failed to
construct in a thread, and passed. =22.locale.cons.mt= reports its
thread pool failing to start. The 15D baseline rows for these tests
record symptoms, not a reference; =doc/notes/working/test-baseline.md=
chapter 3.4 has the details.

A ThreadSanitizer build of 15D names the cause: =numpunct= caches
the results of its virtuals in members of the shared facet with no
synchronization (=include/loc/_numpunct.h:142= on), so two threads
assign the same cached =std::string= and one frees the
representation the other is copying; the facet's implementation
data and the locale body's bookkeeping are initialized the same way.
The facet has a mutex it does not take. One run: 31 data races, 5
use-after-free, 4 destroyed mutexes. Two configuration facts sit
beside it: on Linux/x86-64 the string reference count goes through
a mutex per string for 4.1.x binary compatibility
(=include/rw/_config.h:155=), and =_RWSTD_NO_THREAD_SAFE_LOCALE= is
characterized and read nowhere. =doc/notes/working/locale-mt-race.md= has
the reports and the intended fixes.

=21.string.stdcxx-162= (=tests/regress=) aborts in =15D= on
=sizeof (strref) <= sizeof (SmallRef)=: with =_RWSTD_REENTRANT= and
string atomics disabled, every string body carries its own mutex,
64 bytes against the 24 the test allows. Atomics would satisfy the
test; the mutex is the 4.1.x-compatibility choice above. The row is
this decision's probe and belongs to it, not to the strings entry.

Fix, after the retirement: guard the lazy initialization in every
caching facet and in the facet data with the mutex the facet already
has; decide the string atomics clause; find or retire the
thread-safe-locale consumer.

Check: the ThreadSanitizer build runs every =22.locale.*.mt= test
with no report from the library, three runs each.

The hardware is aarch64 Linux. There =include/rw/_atomic.h= selects
no backend: the GNU builtin branch is gated on an architecture list
that names only i486, x86-64 and ia64, so the chain falls through to
=_RWSTD_NO_ATOMIC_OPS= and every atomic operation becomes a mutex
operation, serializing the paths the hunt wants unfenced. Before the
MT tests run there, the builtin backend needs an aarch64 port: a
characterization of the builtins in place of the architecture list,
since the existing =ATOMIC_OPS.cpp= only characterizes the Windows
=InterlockedIncrement= signature.

* TODO The C++17 floor

The library is C++98 by design and characterizes the compiler rather
than assuming one. The intended step is to raise the implementation
floor to C++17: =nullptr=, =noexcept= in place of the dynamic
exception specifications the library still declares, =static_assert=,
=override=, inline variables for the facet statics, =<type_traits>=
for the hand-rolled traits, and the retirement of the
characterizations for compilers that no longer exist. This is
distinct from the conformance target, what the library provides to
its users, which is a roadmap of its own.

Not before the baseline; the =0= to =nullptr= sweep is one inert
commit on top of the floor change, never sprinkled in ahead of it.
Until then null is the literal =0=.

* TODO Strict ANSI as the rule, the additions outside the standard gone

=_RWSTD_STRICT_ANSI= (=include/rw/_config.h:234=, selected also by
=_RWSTD_NO_EXTENSIONS=) is an opt-in: it undefines =_RWSTD_LONG_LONG=
and defines 30 =_RWSTD_NO_EXT_*= macros, and the original README
calls the switch deprecated. The tree reads 38 distinct
=_RWSTD_NO_EXT_*= names in 40 files under =include= and =src=, eight
of which the strict block never sets, and the tests carry the same
gates; =tests/intro/17.extensions= exercises the additions directly.

Intent: strict conformance becomes the only configuration, and what
leaves the code tree is strictly what the library adds outside the
standard: members, classes, overloads and manipulator values that
ISO/IEC 14882:2003 does not specify, such as the =ios_base::failure=
subclasses, base-2 stream I/O and =ios_base::bin=, the
=stringbuf::str (const char*)= overload, the deep-copy
=basic_string::copy=, the extra =num_get=/=num_put=/=time_get=
members, the =void= =count= and =distance= forms, the =setbase= bases
beyond the required ones, the filebuf and stream-locking additions.
Everything the =_RWSTD_NO_EXT_*= gates cover that is not such an
addition is not part of the trim: an implementation choice the
standard permits (the in-place =assign= and =insert= of =deque= and
=vector=, the width kept on a failed insertion, the portable random
sequence, the primary-template definitions), a later defect
resolution the block disables under the extension name (LWG 559,
cv-qualified =numeric_limits=; LWG 568, the safe stream conversion),
and =long long=, which follows the C++17 floor entry. Those keep
their present behaviour and lose only the macro.

Fix, in order, one subsystem per commit with the suite tables as the
oracle: first the 38 gates sorted into the two lists above, recorded
in the commit that deletes =_RWSTD_STRICT_ANSI= and
=_RWSTD_NO_EXTENSIONS= from =_config.h=; then per addition, the code
path deleted with its macro, its test branches and its
=17.extensions= cases; per kept behaviour, the macro and its dead
branch deleted. The original README stays as it is, =README.md=
records the policy. Sequencing against the C++17 floor to decide
before the first commit.

Check: =git grep _RWSTD_NO_EXT_= empty under =include=, =src= and
=tests=; =17.extensions= reduced to nothing or retired; the suite
tables re-pinned in all six configurations with no row but the
extension rows moving; the examples build.

* Documentation

Documents that describe the library as it is, for a reader rather
than for the record; they go under =doc/notes=, the investigations
under =doc/notes/working=. Each entry names the document, what it
must answer, and where the material already is. None has a check
beyond a reading; they are prose. Roughly in order of payback.

** TODO A tour of the locale subsystem

Facets and their ids, the facet index scheme, =locale_body= and how
=combine= and the global locale share bodies, the byname facets and
the generated binary locale data they read. The MT race lives here
(=doc/notes/working/locale-mt-race.md=) and a reader chasing it needs
the map first. Shape: =string-replace.md=.

** TODO Decision records

One short file per irreversible choice the revival made: the platform
retirement, the null-pointer literal, the commit shape, the Clang
configuration, the compatibility mutex in the string body. The public
tree has only the outcomes; the reasoning is in commit messages and
private notes. Material: the commit messages and
=doc/notes/working/platform-state.md=.

** TODO A conformance record

What of ISO/IEC 14882:2003 the library implements, where it knowingly
deviates, which LWG issues it applied (the headers cite them by
number), and which later library features it lacks. The roadmap the
C++17 floor entry defers to. The most valuable to an outside reader
and the most work.

** TODO A build-system reference

How the =BUILDTYPE= digits and letters map to flags, why =config.h= is
a function of the compiler and =makefile.in= only, how a
characterization is written and how its result gates code, the serial
phases. The original =README= covers 2008; the revival's rules are
spread over =README.md= and the commit messages.

** TODO The characterization catalogue

The probes under =etc/config/src=, each yielding a =_RWSTD_NO_*=
macro: a table of macro, what it detects, which supported compiler
trips it today, and whether it is still reachable. Shows at a glance
what the C++17 floor could retire.

** TODO An ABI and versioning note

What the shared library exports, how the version macros and the
=_RWSTD_VER= checks work, what changing the string body would break.
The 4.1.x compatibility clause that keeps the per-string mutex is a
binary-compatibility decision with no document behind it.

** TODO A test-suite guide

How the driver's =rw_assert= and =rw_test= work, the =TEST= table
idiom and its =bthrow= column, how the harness classifies rows, how
to write a regression against a table test. Fragments exist in
=doc/notes/working/test-baseline.md=.

** TODO A threading model note

What the library promises under =_RWSTD_REENTRANT=, what the string's
reference counting does and does not protect, the facet caching the
ThreadSanitizer run named. The front matter for the MT entry.

** TODO A tour of the streams

The =basic_streambuf= buffering contract, the =ios_base= storage, how
=filebuf= maps onto the C library. Shape: =string-replace.md=.
